<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Khatisian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alexandre Khatisian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Khatisian was an Armenian politician of the Dashnak party, formerly mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>. He served as prime minister of the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from 1919 to 1920 and signed the Treaty of Alexandropol with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in December 1920, shortly before the Soviet takeover. He died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-khatisian.jpg" alt="Alexander Khatisian" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Léon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1921_Alexander_Khatisian.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Khatisian was born on 17 February 1874 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, in the Tiflis Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to a prominent Armenian family of noble origins. After a state school in Tiflis he trained as a doctor at universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and he also worked as a journalist. As mayor of Tiflis from 1910 to 1917 he was consulted in the summer of 1914 by Count Illarion Ivanovich Vorontsov-Dashkov on forming Armenian volunteer detachments, which he enthusiastically supported and organised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Khatisian joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a> in 1917 and sat for the Armenian National Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> on the Armenian National Council. After the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> was declared he served as foreign minister and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-batum">Treaty of Batum</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He was later interior minister after the death of Aram Manukian, and resigned as prime minister in the aftermath of the Bolshevik-led May Uprising of 1920.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s first prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hovhannes-kajaznuni">Hovhannes Kajaznuni</a>, praised his inexhaustible energy and administrative experience but complained that he had no moral authority and did not inspire confidence. After resigning, Khatisian sought aid for the republic in European capitals. On 3 December 1920 a delegation he led signed the Treaty of Alexandropol with Kemalist Turkey, although power had already passed to the new Soviet authorities, so that the government it represented no longer existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Soviet takeover of Armenia Khatisian went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. There he wrote his memoirs, The Memoirs of a Mayor, and a History and Development of the Republic of Armenia, published in 1930 and revised in 1968. Arrested after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-paris">liberation of Paris</a> on suspicion of collaborating with the Germans, he was soon released, and he died in the city on 10 March 1945 at the age of 71.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Khatisian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Yerevan government sent <b>Alexander Khatisian</b> to negotiate with the Turks and appointed a team under General Drastamat Kanayan to hand power to the Bolsheviks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic was governed by the Armenian Revolutionary Federation, with prime ministers Hovhannes Kajaznuni and <b>Alexander Khatisian</b> and a parliament elected by universal suffrage, including women.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in 17th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Tbilisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5641 677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander Khatisian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.72,44.79&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
